<CaseStudyLayout
  title="PitLogic"
  description="Case study: client-side BBQ telemetry, alerts, history, and cook analytics."
  eyebrow="Product engineering · Telemetry"
  lede="A long cook produces a ridiculous amount of temperature data. I do not need more graphs just because graphs are available; I need to know what is happening, whether dinner is in trouble, and what happened the last time I cooked this cut."
  status="Active"
  access="Open source · MIT"
  tags={['React', 'PWA', 'Telemetry', 'Analytics']}
  sections={['The problem', 'The product shape', 'The backend I did not build', 'What I like about this one']}
  tone="copper"
  visual="pitlogic"
  repoUrl="https://github.com/unyieldingclaw-dev/pitlogic"
  liveUrl="https://unyieldingclaw-dev.github.io/pitlogic/"
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Long cooks create lots of data but still leave the cook answering practical questions: Is the pit cooling off? Is the meat entering a stall? Am I likely to finish on time? What happened the last time I cooked this cut?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The product shape</h2>
    <p>
      The application stays client-side instead of introducing accounts and a backend that the use case does not require. It combines active-cook monitoring with history and analytics, while keeping the data portable.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Live ThermoWorks RFX data arrives through MQTT over WebSocket.</li>
      <li>Target and low-pit alarms surface actionable conditions instead of just drawing graphs.</li>
      <li>Stall detection and finish-time estimates turn telemetry into decisions.</li>
      <li>Cook history, comparisons, recipes, and JSON backup make the data useful after the cook ends.</li>
      <li>PWA and accessibility work keep the product usable on the devices and in the situations where it actually gets used.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="case-callout">
    <h2>The backend I did not build</h2>
    <p>
      There is no backend because I could not find a good reason to make people create an account to watch a brisket. Local storage, export/import, and direct device integration do the job with less infrastructure and fewer ways for Saturday dinner to depend on my cloud bill.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I like about this one</h2>
    <p>
      PitLogic is refreshingly not an AI project. It is live data, alarms, forecasting, accessibility, testing, deployment, and knowing when to stop building infrastructure. Sometimes the right architecture is the one that lets me get back to the smoker.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
